    "gloss": "All good and bad will pass away; this boat will cross and arrive",
    "literal": "Good and bad both will pass. This boat will just cross over.",
    "poetic":
        "All that's good and all that's bad\nWill pass, the journey goes on.\nThis boat will cross and arrive,\nNo matter how long it takes to go on.",
    "notes":
        ["The 'good' and 'bad' are a stock phrase from the ghazal tradition. Hali uses it here as a metaphor for all kinds of experiences that come and go in life, which is why he says they will pass away. 'This boat' refers to the human journey through life, which must cross over (par) to arrive at its destination (utar). The wordplay on the two meanings of 'par' is intentional: it means both 'cross over' and 'arrive'. Hali's use of the present tense in the first line reflects his Sufi belief that all experiences are part of a continuous journey, which is why he uses the future tense for the boat arriving at its destination. The wordplay on 'yūn' (just) and 'yūn' (thus), as well as the use of the present tense, make it clear that Hali is not making any promises about how long this journey will take; it's just going to keep going on."
